# chat

Interactive terminal REPL front-end — the Claude-CLI-style daily driver.
Additive: reuses `run_single` / the tool surface / the prompt registry
unchanged. The deterministic benchmark path (`luxe maintain`, `benchmarks/`)
never touches this module.

## Must
- `chat/launch.py` owns the STARTUP path of `luxe chat` / `luxe code`
  (2026-08-04 lift out of cli.py, behavior unchanged): the shared Click option
  list, `_run_interactive` (resolve project → bounded index → repo lock → MCP
  → front-end selection → teardown), `_build_chat_indexes` + its
  `LUXE_INDEX_MAX_*` bounds, `_resolve_theme_name`, `_apply_slot_overrides`.
  `cli.chat_cmd` / `cli.code_cmd` are two-line shells that differ ONLY in the
  `require_project` / `start_write` posture, and `cli` re-exports every moved
  private so `luxe.cli._X` keeps resolving. `cli` imports this module; this
  module imports `cli` only INSIDE functions (`_resolve_repo`,
  `_default_chat_config`, `_default_mcp_config_hint`,
  `_languages_from_paths`) — never at module level, or the two would deadlock
  on import, and the late binding is what keeps `monkeypatch.setattr(cli, …)`
  behaving as before
- Every user turn drives exactly ONE `run_single()` call; conversation state
  lives in `ChatSession`, folded into `goal=` + a tagged `extra_context` block —
  never by forking `run_agent`'s message loop or changing its signature
- Context precedence (highest wins): current user turn > project memory >
  conversation summary. `extra_context` is tagged `<project_memory>` /
  `<conversation_history>` / `<current_request>` so history can't read as commands
- Default slot config = THIS HOST'S manifest `main` in chat/plan/code
  (2026-07-30 fallback-kit pivot): `model_for_slot` resolves an empty
  `model_key` to `hosts:<hostname>`.main first, then to the monolith model.
  A host with no `hosts:` entry keeps champion-everywhere. Explicit `slots:`,
  CLI `--<slot>-model`, and `/model` overrides always beat the manifest.
  Benchmark/maintain never call `model_for_slot`/`host_manifest`, so
  `single_64gb.yaml` selection is untouched
- Per-host manifests (`hosts:` in configs/chat.yaml, keyed by short hostname,
  parsed as HostManifest main/fallback/keep/ctx_max) are a DISK declaration as
  well as a selection default. `ctx_max` is a per-MODEL `/ctx` ceiling (KV
  cost differs ~3× across the Qwen3.6 family: dense 64 KB/token vs MoE 20);
  `SlotManager.ctx_ceiling` returns min(role num_ctx_max, the cap for the
  model the slot CURRENTLY resolves to — overrides and auto-degrade
  included), and `prepare_turn` clamps `/ctx` overrides through it every
  turn, so a session degraded to the dense fallback shrinks to the dense cap
  on its next turn. Also: all three sets are expected locally cached on that host.
  `luxe pull` provisions, `/doctor` + `luxe smoke` verify (including the
  dangling-symlink state a wiped HF cache leaves behind), and
  `luxe pull <name> --remove` refuses manifest models without --force.
  Fleet 2026-07-30: m5 = 35B-A3B-6bit / 27B-6bit; m1 + m4 = 35B-A3B-4bit /
  27B-4bit (MoE main — Qwen3.6 is multimodal, oMLX serves it on the slow vlm
  engine; dense-27B prefill is ~65 tok/s with no cache reuse, MoE ~10s turns);
  m1 also keeps the bench champion (keep:). neo (8 GB) has no entry on purpose
- AUTO-DEGRADE is loud and single-fire: when the manifest main (a) is missing
  from the server catalog at first `backend_for`, (b) fails the post-swap
  thermal_guard, or (c) throws a turn-level BackendError while the endpoint
  reports healthy (oMLX lazy-loads, so missing/corrupt weights surface at
  request time — `SlotManager.note_turn_failure`), every default resolution of
  main reroutes to the manifest fallback, announced via on_status (both
  front-ends render it), surfaced in `/doctor` as a WARN, and never silently:
  a session running a different model than asked is the failure mode this
  kills. Manual `/model` overrides bypass the reroute; an endpoint-down
  BackendError does NOT degrade (that's a backend problem, `/backend` is the
  escape hatch)
- Multi-slot fan-out activates ONLY on explicit `configs/chat.yaml` `slots:` —
  the sanctioned, opt-in exception to luxe.sdd "no per-task model selection",
  scoped to this front-end
- `luxe code` is the same engine as `luxe chat` with a POSTURE preset (shared
  body `cli._run_interactive`; option surfaces must not drift — one
  `_shared_chat_options` list): it REQUIRES a project (resolve.kind "none" →
  exit 2 with the fix, never a degraded session) and starts with
  `write_enabled=True`. Bash stays gated. The persona rules above are
  UNCHANGED in code posture — freeform turns stay conversational
- Every session writes `<session dir>/debug.log` (chat/debuglog.py): a DEBUG
  FileHandler on the `luxe` package logger, installed once the session id
  exists, removed in the front-end finally. File-only by design — the TUI owns
  the screen and previously ate every logger.error traceback. Failed turns
  also persist a kind="error" transcript record (rendered by `/export`,
  ignored by resume pairing)
- `luxe smoke` (chat/smoke.py) is the aliveness drill: manifest resolved,
  weights real on disk, endpoint up, oMLX build not stale, catalog complete,
  one real generation + one tool call on main, one generation on the fallback
  (exercises the swap).
  Minutes, not a bench; exit 0/1; run it after provisioning and on a schedule.
  The `oMLX build` step (`luxe.staleproc`, shared with `/doctor` — same
  invariants, see the `/doctor` bullet) is diagnostic and WARN-only: it must
  never flip the exit code on its own, because its job is to EXPLAIN the turn
  failures below it, not to add a new way for a working host to go red. It is
  positioned directly after `endpoint` for that reason.
  `--code` / `--chat` run the AGENTIC drills instead (combinable): real
  run_single turns against a planted scratch git repo — --code plants a
  one-line bug + failing test and verifies the fix DETERMINISTICALLY (pytest
  rc + git diff --name-only == calc.py, never by trusting the model's word);
  --chat is a read-only turn that must read a file to recover a magic word
  that exists nowhere in the prompt. Drill models resolve from the manifest
  of the host the ENDPOINT points at (`--backend m5` must run an m5 model,
  not this host's pair); an aborted run_single surfaces its abort_reason as
  the failure; the scratch repo is kept on failure (path in the report) and
  removed on success; a remote `--backend` drill never unloads that server's
  models
- Slot routing is visible: the render footer shows the fired slot + model every
  turn; `/use <slot>` and `/model <slot> <id>` are explicit user overrides.
  `/model all <n|id>` (2026-08-03) pins every slot at once — the supported way
  to run a whole session on one non-default model (e.g. the m5 capacity
  model), since keyword slot-routing otherwise lets a code-flavored message
  land on an unpinned slot's model
- EVERY freeform interactive turn uses the CONVERSATIONAL persona:
  `prepare_turn` swaps the role's `system_prompt_id`/`task_prompt_id` to the
  registry variant `chat_conversational` (and clears the task overlay) so chat
  answers directly instead of running the code-maintenance orientation loop (a
  bare "hello" used to `list_dir` + read the README + emit a "final report").
  The swap keys on turn KIND, NOT on the routed slot — slot routing comes from
  the `_infer_task_type` keyword heuristic (built for `luxe maintain` goals),
  and keying the persona on `slot == "chat"` was the 2026-07-29 "chats become
  coding sessions" bug: ordinary messages containing "add"/"change"/"explain"/
  "fix"/… routed to the code/plan slots and inherited the maintenance persona.
  The baseline task personas apply ONLY to the explicit task modes: `/plan`
  drafting (`plan_mode`), autonomous `/goal` rounds (`session.goal_active`),
  and a `/use <slot>`-pinned turn. Slot routing still selects the MODEL
  (fan-out configs unchanged); the conversational persona keeps the full tool
  surface and does real work when asked. The string lives in the prompt
  registry (never inline in this module)
- Backend switching is CHAT-ONLY and CONFIG-SOURCED (luxe.sdd carve-out): the
  `backends:` map (BackendEntry = base_url / api_key_env / timeout_s /
  stall_timeout_s / decode_stall_timeout_s / shared / default; the last two are the B6
  progress deadlines, `None` = inherit Backend's default, forwarded via
  `BackendEntry.backend_kwargs()` which OMITS unset keys rather than passing
  None)
  is the sole registry of endpoints — `/backend` lists entries (name, base_url,
  health ✓/✗, active marker) and `/backend <name|n>` switches; `--backend
  <name>` picks the startup entry. API keys are NEVER in YAML: `api_key_env`
  names the env var, resolved at Backend construction (empty → Backend's
  OMLX_API_KEY fallback). `SlotManager.switch_backend` health-checks the new
  endpoint FIRST (unreachable → BackendError, session stays put), drops `/model`
  overrides whose ids don't resolve on the new server (reported to the user),
  resets the resident model so the next turn re-confirms weights there, and
  NEVER unloads models on the old server (it may be the endpoint that just went
  down). A turn failing with BackendError keeps the REPL/TUI alive and — only
  when >1 backend is configured — prints the `/backend` escape hatch (e.g.
  "local oMLX unreachable — try /backend m5"). ANY other turn/command
  exception is contained the same way (last traceback line to the transcript,
  full traceback to the logger, session stays up) — a crashed turn is a
  message, never an exit. Containing only BackendError is what let an
  `OSError(ETIMEDOUT)` from a repo walk kill the TUI on 2026-07-29. The status bar gains a `backend`
  segment (mid drop-priority) only when >1 backend is configured; configs
  without `backends:` synthesize a single "local" entry and behave identically
  to the single-backend days. Benchmark/maintain read `omlx_base_url` only.
- SINGLE-RESIDENCY POLICY (2026-07-30, user decision): ONE model in RAM per
  host — including the 128 GB m5, whose headroom is reserved for CONTEXT,
  not warm spares. The swap path always unloaded-except-target;
  `backend_for`'s first use now evicts other residents even on the no-swap
  short-circuit (re-armed on `switch_backend` for the new server), and the
  smoke pings + drills unload-except before generating. Cost accepted: a
  manual `/model` hop to the other manifest model pays a full unload+load
- Different-model slot use sequences weight swaps via
  `Backend.unload_all_loaded` + `thermal_guard`; swap count + seconds are
  instrumented and surfaced; keep chat-slot model resident, group code work
- The history summarizer (`chat/summarize.py`) is NON-model, deterministic, and
  versioned (`SUMMARIZER_VERSION`); its output is persisted to `fold.jsonl`
- Cancel during a turn cancels MID-GENERATION: the CancelToken is set (the line
  REPL via a SIGINT handler; the TUI via the `escape`/`ctrl+c` key binding) and
  the streaming `on_token` callback (chat-only, `stream=True`) raises
  `ChatCancelled` (a KeyboardInterrupt, so loop.py's `except Exception` can't
  swallow it) at the next token — not only at tool boundaries. Persists the
  partial transcript and returns to the prompt (no process exit). In the TUI,
  cancel ALSO dismisses any open `PromptScreen` modal so a blocked worker
  unwinds. Responsiveness is bounded by the backend's token-streaming cadence.
  An in-flight chat `bash` subprocess is KILLED (2026-07-31): `prepare_turn`
  threads the CancelToken into `make_bash_fn(cancel=…)`, whose Popen runner
  polls the token (~0.2s) and kills the whole process group — previously esc
  blocked on `subprocess.run` for up to the 600s dev budget (session
  5bb630813c21: a hung curl made cancel a ten-minute wait). The
  benchmark/maintain path (`on_token=None`, `stream=False`, module
  `TOOL_FNS["bash"]`, `cancel=None`) is unaffected and keeps tool-boundary
  cancellation.
- An interrupted turn persists what actually happened (2026-07-31):
  `finalize_turn` records the tool calls the front-end OBSERVED
  (`TurnPrep.observed_calls` — the in-flight AgentResult is lost when
  ChatCancelled unwinds) and the streamed prose so far (`partial_text`), so
  "partial turn saved" is true — the record used to be
  steps=0/tool_calls=0/text="" no matter what ran.
- Tool dispatch is visible AT DISPATCH, not only on completion (2026-07-31):
  loop.py logs `tool dispatch …name/args` + `tool done` + per-step
  `ctx_pressure_est` at DEBUG (chat's debug.log captures them; the bench
  configures no handler so stdout/events.jsonl are unchanged), `finalize_turn`
  logs the server-truth ctx% per turn, the line REPL prints a dim `$ <cmd>`
  line when bash STARTS, and the TUI activity line names the currently
  executing command via `make_bash_fn(on_start=…)` — a hung command
  identifies itself instead of hiding behind counts of finished calls.
- The per-turn pipeline is UI-agnostic: `prepare_turn` (slot/role/tool/ledger
  setup + the verbatim `run_single` closure) and `finalize_turn` (persistence +
  `TurnOutcome`) are shared by BOTH front-ends; `run_single`'s call args are
  assembled identically so the benchmark path stays byte-identical. Interactive
  sub-prompts (/plan choice, gitkit clone URL, compare vote) go through one
  `reader`/`prompt_user` seam — `console.input` on the line path, a modal on the
  TUI. `_run_plan`/`_run_goal_loop` take injectable `run_turn`/`reader` so the
  supervisor logic is single-sourced.
- Working-state ledger (`state/ledger.py`, persisted to
  `~/.luxe/sessions/<id>/ledger.json`): a compact
  decided/completed/in_progress/blocked/files record injected as a high-precedence
  `<working_state>` block by `build_extra_context` so `continue work`/`/goal`
  rounds consult known state instead of re-reading plan.md + all sources. Two
  feeds: deterministic (write/edit paths observed from the tool-event stream) and
  model-driven (the always-exposed `update_ledger` tool, wired via run_single's
  extra-tool seam). Empty ledger → byte-identical legacy context (no block)
- `/verbose [diff|full|off]` controls tool-IO visibility and `/reasoning` toggles
  live streaming of model prose — INDEPENDENT session flags (reasoning is often
  low-signal; never coupled). Verbose reuses the `on_tool_event` seam
  (`format_tool_call_verbose`): `diff` renders edit_file as a unified diff +
  write_file headers + capped result bodies + the ledger view; `full` dumps whole
  files. All content is Rich-escaped and bounded. Reasoning rides the same
  streaming `on_token` seam as Ctrl-C, line-buffered so it scrolls above the Live
  region. Both default off → unchanged one-line summaries
- `/goal <objective>` is an autonomous supervisor in the REPL loop: round 1 sends
  the objective, later rounds send `continue work`, each printing `[goal round
  N/M]`. Termination keys on OBSERVABLE state (iteration-5, C1/D1/D6): a "settled"
  round (files_changed==0) is DONE when it shows GREEN tests (parsed from tool
  output by `parse_test_result` — passed>0, zero failed/errors) OR carries
  `LUXE_GOAL_DONE` + a grown ledger `completed`, for 2 consecutive rounds. The
  green-tests path completes a finished run even if the model never logged
  `completed` or signaled (the iter-5 run-2/3 false-STUCK); the sentinel path alone
  never suffices (keeps the 32K false-done shut). It does NOT require `in_progress`
  empty. STUCK has two guards: idle (settled rounds, no new completed, ≥3) and
  THRASH (rounds that ran tests without reducing failures and added no completed,
  ≥3 — catches the edit→test→same-failures loop; no-test rounds don't count, so
  staged work isn't punished). Completion is checked first and resets both. The
  STUCK message reports observable state (files built + last test result), never
  "completed=0 … likely NOT done". Crash resilience retries on CONSECUTIVE failures;
  a tool-fingerprint guard is a faster stuck trip; a ≤32K window prints a
  low-context advisory. The pure `evaluate_goal_round()` + `parse_test_result()`
  are unit-tested. Requires write mode;
  Ctrl-C or `/goal stop` halts it
- `/plan <objective>` drafts a plan in a forced READ-ONLY turn (PLAN_HINT, reusing
  make_read_only_role) then prompts save / execute / both / discard. Save never
  clobbers an existing `plan.md` (writes `.luxe/plans/<id>-<n>.md`); execute seeds
  the `/goal` runner and injects the drafted plan as a `<plan>` provenance block
  so the agent keeps following it
- `/attach <path> [...]` stages file contents for the NEXT turn, ONE-SHOT:
  read at command time (expanduser'd; binary refused via a null-byte sniff of
  the first 8KB; capped 48KB/file + 128KB/turn-total with an explicit
  truncation marker), staged on `ChatSession.attachments`, injected by
  `build_extra_context` as an `<attached_files>` block sitting JUST BELOW
  `<system_constraints>` in the precedence ladder (the user's explicit rules
  outrank pasted content; attachments outrank style/plan/ledger/memory/
  history), then cleared on consumption — later turns recall it only via
  conversation history. Each attach appends a kind="attachment" transcript
  record (path, size, sha256, truncated, capped content); resume pairing
  ignores unknown kinds, so old readers are unaffected
- `/terse` (default ON) injects the registry `TERSE_HINT` `<response_style>` block
  to cut model prose ("report only deltas") — carved out so it never abbreviates
  tool output, errors, or skips needed reads/confirmations. `/debug` is a
  convenience that flips `/verbose full` + `/reasoning` together. `/verbose` now
  renders code via rich `Syntax` and edits as highlighted diffs, with a crash-safe
  plain-text fallback
- The chat palette tracks the user's theme: tool lines, status bar, ledger,
  banner, and prompt arrows resolve colors from `theme.role_styles()` roles
  (`accent`/`success`/`error`/`warn`/`slot`/`diff_*`/…) instead of hardcoded hex —
  ANSI-named fallbacks track the terminal profile, and the `slot` segment no
  longer hardcodes magenta. `auto` (default) tracks the YASL/terminal theme;
  `--theme cool|warm|mono` (C-T) selects a curated luxe palette (256/RGB) that
  overrides terminal ANSI — needed because a standard-index YASL theme equals the
  ANSI fallback, so auto looks unchanged. `/theme <name>` PERSISTS the choice
  to `~/.luxe/theme` (2026-07-30) so future sessions start with it; startup
  precedence is `--theme` > `LUXE_THEME` > persisted > `auto`, an explicit
  flag/env always beating the saved default. `/theme preview` renders the real
  status bar + tool/diff lines in every palette without changing anything
- Goal completion is SENTINEL-required + ledger-corroborated (C1): a settled round
  is done only when `completed` is non-empty, grew at least once this run, and the
  reply carries `LUXE_GOAL_DONE` — for 2 consecutive rounds. It does NOT require
  `in_progress` empty (models leave stale plan-phase headers → the iter-4 a5812
  false-STUCK). Completion is checked before stuck and resets the stuck counter;
  `in_progress` pruning/clearing is cosmetic provenance, never control flow. The
  per-round decision is the pure `evaluate_goal_round()` (unit-tested)
- Context % is one metric end-to-end (C2): `AgentResult.final_context_pressure`
  (last per-step instantaneous, via the chat-only `on_progress` callback) drives
  both the live and between-turn status bar, matching the `[token-progress]` line;
  the footer shows `ctx(peak)` separately. `huge` (256K) is the new `/ctx` ceiling,
  clamped by `num_ctx_max`; the startup banner shows the git build via
  `buildinfo.version_string()`, and `luxe chat` gains
  `--verbose/--show-reasoning/--no-terse/--debug/--theme` startup flags
- `/pull` fetches model weights (2026-07-29) — mounted volume first (LAN copy),
  HuggingFace second (oMLX's downloader via `modelstore.OmlxAdmin`; never a
  second downloader of our own). Consent is two-step in chat: `/pull <ref>`
  PREVIEWS source + size, `/pull <ref> --yes` transfers — a slash command has
  no confirmation prompt and a pull can move tens of GB. It is a COMMAND, never
  a tool: the model cannot initiate a download. Same core as `luxe pull`.
  SOURCE RESOLUTION is literally shared (2026-08-04): both surfaces call
  `modelstore.resolve_pull_sources(ref, admin=, from_path=, include_mounts=)`
  and `tests/test_modelstore.py::TestPullSourceParity` asserts they reach it
  with the same arguments, so the two can never disagree about where a ref
  comes from. What is NOT shared, deliberately: the consent shape (chat
  previews then `--yes`; the CLI prompts `click.confirm`), progress rendering
  (chat prints one line per 10%, the CLI drives a Rich bar), the wording of
  every message, and the CLI-only `--list`/`--remove`/HF-cache materialize
  steps. Those are two different UIs, not duplication — do not fold them.
- Session-shape commands (2026-07-29 `/help` audit): `/theme` switches the
  palette live (previously startup-only, so the shipped default was
  unescapable), `/tools` prints the ACTUAL tool surface for the next turn with
  read-only-gated tools listed separately, `/status` is the one-screen session
  dump (incl. model origin), `/unload` frees oMLX RAM mid-session (and must call
  `SlotManager.forget_resident()` so the next turn reloads), `/retry` re-runs the
  last user message via `CommandResult.submit` — the only sanctioned way a
  command turns into a turn; both front-ends act on that field. `/copy`
  (2026-07-31) puts the LAST answer on the system clipboard via the platform
  tool (pbcopy/wl-copy/xclip/xsel; never OSC 52 — the alternate-screen TUI
  owns the tty) — the TUI captures the mouse and RichLog has no selection, so
  this + `/export` are the sanctioned copy-out paths. A leading-`/` line whose
  first token is NOT a known command and looks like a filesystem path (second
  `/`, or exists on disk) is dispatched as a MESSAGE (`commands.is_command`),
  so a pasted absolute path is sendable; path-free unknown tokens still get
  the unknown-command error (typo protection).
- Network diagnostics live in `src/luxe/netdiag.py` (2026-07-31), NEVER as
  more `/doctor` lines (doctor's offline-purity contract allows exactly one
  networked check). Three surfaces, one implementation: the `net_probe` tool
  (rides every chat turn via the extra-tool seam, like update_ledger —
  read-only, in-process, every layer hard-bounded, so the model reaches for
  it instead of hand-rolling curl commands with no timeout — the session
  5bb630813c21 failure); `/net [host]` + `luxe net` (deterministic report:
  DNS→TCP→TLS→HTTP(S) ladder + captive-portal check + every configured
  `backends:` endpoint via GET /health); `luxe net --watch N` (re-probe on an
  interval, print verdict TRANSITIONS only, append to ~/.luxe/netwatch.log).
  The ladder ends in a CLASSIFIED verdict (offline / dns-broken / tcp-blocked
  / captive-portal / tls-intercepted / tls-blocked / degraded / ok) — the
  classifier is pure and unit-tested; probes are tested against local
  synthetic servers (silent-TCP handshake timeout, self-signed TLS, portal
  redirect). No probe may block past its deadline (DNS runs on an abandoned
  daemon thread — getaddrinfo has no timeout parameter). The benchmark/
  maintain path never touches this module.
- planeproxy diagnostics live in `src/luxe/planeproxy.py` (2026-08-02), same
  three-surface shape as netdiag: the `planeproxy_diag` tool (rides every chat
  turn via the extra-tool seam), `/planeproxy [status|doctor]` in chat, and
  `luxe planeproxy` CLI. Every probe is BOUNDED (subprocess deadline; a hang
  becomes an error string) and READ-ONLY: the module runs only the tool's own
  `status --json`/`doctor --json` and MUST NEVER run `up`/`down`/`on`/`off` —
  starting or stopping the tunnel is a user action (mutations are commands
  with consent flows, and no such command exists by design). The classifier
  is pure and unit-tested; a missing binary is a structured `not-installed`
  verdict, never an error (analysis.py B3). Doctrine the surfaces must never
  contradict: a host-key mismatch is a STOP (the network is intercepting),
  never a retry, and no surface — advice string, tool description, or prompt
  hint — may ever suggest installing a network's CA certificate or disabling
  certificate verification.
- There is EXACTLY ONE web/browser stack: `src/luxe/web/` (see web.sdd).
  Consolidated 2026-08-03 from two implementations that landed in parallel
  the same week — `src/luxe/browser.py` (`browse_navigate`/`browse_read` over
  headless-Chrome CDP, always-on, `[browser]` extra = pychrome + trafilatura)
  was REMOVED in favour of `web_fetch`/`web_search`, and its two good ideas
  were ported rather than lost: an optional fnmatch host allowlist
  (`LUXE_WEB_ALLOWLIST`, deny-by-default WHEN SET, unset = any public host)
  and a system-Chrome fallback so `playwright install chromium` is an
  optimisation, not a requirement. Do not reintroduce a second stack, a
  second browser dependency, or a second gating model: one surface, one
  guard, one extra. The allowlist LAYERS on the IP-class egress guard and
  does not replace it — an allowlist says nothing about where a name
  resolves, so it cannot stop the tailnet/CGNAT case on its own.
- A session can start ANYWHERE (2026-07-30). `luxe/project.py` (moved out of
  chat/ 2026-08-05, deferred #5 — gitkit's brief needs the same resolution and
  the old home forced a gitkit→chat import; `chat/project.py` remains as a
  re-export shim for the historical import path) resolves the
  subject once at startup: **git** (cwd is in a work tree → the project is the
  GIT ROOT, so a subdir session sees the whole repo), **dir** (no git but a
  marker: pyproject.toml/package.json/…), or **none**. `$HOME` and any directory
  ABOVE it are never the project (a dotfiles repo at `~` must not become one); a
  repo outside home is fine. `--repo` resolves upward too — the `luxe-chat`
  wrapper always passes `--repo "$PWD"`, so treating an explicit flag as "pin
  here" would defeat walk-up for the primary entry point.
  In **none** mode: no index is built, no repo lock is taken (there's no codebase
  to protect, and locking `$HOME` would block a second chat window), read tools
  still work against cwd, `bm25_search`/`find_symbol` are WITHHELD from the tool
  list (derived from `search/symbols.get_index()`, so `/index` re-enables them
  with no other bookkeeping), and the prompt carries `NO_PROJECT_CHAT_HINT` so
  the model says "not indexed here", never "luxe can't search". The status bar
  shows `no project`.
- `/project [path]` and `/index [path]` attach or switch mid-session via the
  `CommandContext.on_project` hook, which cli owns because cli owns the lock:
  it acquires the NEW lock before releasing the old one, so a `LockHeld` leaves
  the session exactly where it was. The hook re-indexes, re-points
  `set_repo_root`, invalidates the `.sdd` scan cache, and returns a summary the
  command renders; both front-ends then update `session.repo_path` /
  `project_kind` / `index_head`.
- Startup indexing is ONE bounded scan (2026-07-30). `cli._build_chat_indexes`
  calls `fswalk.scan_source_files` once and passes the file list to BOTH
  `build_bm25_index(files=…)` and `build_symbol_index(files=…)`; languages come
  from the same list (`_languages_from_paths`), never a third walk. The scan
  uses `git ls-files` when the root is a repo (so `.gitignore` does the pruning),
  is breadth-first otherwise (a cap should keep the SHALLOW files), prunes
  `HOME_NOISE_DIRS` at depth 1 only, and is bounded by `LUXE_INDEX_MAX_FILES`
  (8000) / `LUXE_INDEX_MAX_MB` (96). Truncation is never silent: startup names
  the cap, what the model can no longer see, and both escapes. Chat-only — the
  builders' no-`files` path is the unchanged unbounded walk the benchmarks use.
- SESSION WORKING NOTES (`chat/notes.py`, 2026-08-04): luxe remembers what it
  did. On session end — the `finally` of BOTH front-ends, BEFORE the model
  unload so the backend is still usable — and on demand via `/note`, ONE
  non-agentic `backend.chat` call (never `run_single`; no tools are needed)
  distils the DETERMINISTIC FOLD of the conversation (`chat/summarize.py`,
  never the raw transcript) into 3–6 bullets using the registry's
  `SESSION_NOTES_HINT`, RECOVERS the bullet list from the reply in Python
  (`extract_bullets` — the last contiguous run of COLUMN-0 bullets; the
  champion narrates a "thinking process" first and will not be prompted out
  of it, same finding as `deep._heuristic_findings` and
  `brief.strip_preamble`), and splices them into the `luxe:notes` block of
  `<repo>/.luxe/memory.md`. Caps and window are enforced in PYTHON, never by
  the prompt: 900 chars/entry, newest-first, newest 5 entries / 1,500 chars
  total, oldest dropped on write. Skips when: no project attached, fewer than
  2 answered turns, or `notes: false` in chat.yaml — `/note` bypasses the
  last two, since asking for it IS the consent. FAILURE IS A SILENT SKIP with
  NO RETRY and it must never block exit: the guard catches `BaseException`
  (Ctrl-C mid-call is a `KeyboardInterrupt`, anyio cancels are worse) and
  only `SystemExit` propagates. One line is printed when written, naming the
  disable switch. Writing `.luxe/memory.md` from a READ-ONLY session is
  SANCTIONED and must not be "fixed" later as a gate bypass: it is luxe's own
  state file, not the user's code, and the write is orchestrator-side Python
  rather than an agent tool — same precedent as `gitkit.store.mirror_to_repo`
  and `luxe init`'s brief. Benchmark/maintain never import this module.
- `/init` (and `luxe init`) drafts the repo's ORIENTATION BRIEF into the
  `luxe:brief` block of `.luxe/memory.md` — one read-only gitkit pass
  (`gitkit/brief.py`, `GIT_BRIEF_HINT`; see gitkit.sdd). In-session it reuses
  the session's live backend so a `--backend m5` session doesn't silently
  drift to the local endpoint. `--dry-run` prints instead of writing.
  Together with notes, `.luxe/memory.md` now carries TWO machine-managed
  fenced blocks whose markers are load-bearing (memory.sdd); everything else
  in that file is the user's and survives byte-for-byte.
- The command HANDLERS live in six `chat/cmd_*.py` group modules (2026-08-04
  split, behavior unchanged): `cmd_models` (`/model` `/backend` `/pull`
  `/unload`), `cmd_diag` (`/status` `/tools` `/doctor` `/outage` `/net`
  `/planeproxy`), `cmd_transcript` (`/diff` `/export` `/full` `/copy`),
  `cmd_agentic` (`/goal` `/plan` `/attach` `/sys`), `cmd_project` (`/project`
  `/index` `/init` `/note` `/memory` `/gitaudit` `/gitchange` `/compare`), and
  `cmd_toggles` (`/theme` `/use` `/ctx` + the on/off switches). `commands.py`
  keeps the CONTRACT — `CommandResult`/`CommandContext`, `_HELP_ROWS`,
  `_HIDDEN_COMMANDS`, `is_command`, `dispatch`, `_build_handlers`, `_usage`,
  and the four handlers that belong to the dispatcher itself (`/help`
  `/retry` `/resume` `/clear` `/quit`). Direction is one-way: group modules
  import `commands`, and `_build_handlers` imports THEM function-locally, so
  the registry stays lazy and the import graph acyclic. A new command adds a
  handler to a group module and a row to `_HELP_ROWS` — nothing else.
- `cmd_toggles._toggle(attr, name, …)` builds the pure bool-toggle handlers
  (`/write` `/bash` `/reasoning` `/terse` `/compact`) from their differing
  words. Styles are PER-COMMAND parameters, not a convention (`/terse` is
  green when ON because terse is the safe default). `/web` and `/debug` are
  deliberately NOT built from it — one prints a per-provider report, the
  other drives two fields off a compound predicate.
- `commands._usage(ctx, "/cmd")` prints a usage line from `_HELP_ROWS` so the
  help table and the error can't drift. It applies only where the whole usage
  IS the table's args cell (`/plan` `/attach` `/planeproxy` `/compare`); the
  subcommand-specific lines (`/sys add <rule>`, `/memory promote <id>`,
  `/goal <objective>  ·  /goal stop`, `/pull --search <query>`,
  `/use chat|plan|code`, `/verbose … (current: X)`) say more than the table
  can and stay hand-written. Do not force them through the helper — the
  output would change.
- The COMMAND SURFACE IS PARITY-CHECKED (2026-08-04). `_build_handlers()` and
  `_HELP_ROWS` are two independent lists, so `tests/test_chat_commands.py`
  asserts handlers ≡ help-row names ∪ `_HIDDEN_COMMANDS` — a declared
  allowlist of short/back-compat aliases. A new command that never reached
  `/help` fails the suite instead of shipping invisible; hiding one is a
  one-line declaration, never an accident. `dispatch` on an unknown `/foo`
  names the nearest handler (`difflib.get_close_matches`) before falling back
  to "Try /help": under pressure a typo must not cost a round trip.
- EVERY user-visible refusal names its UNLOCK — the `restricted_hint` pattern
  generalised (2026-08-04 audit). Already-good: restricted bash, read-only
  writes, `/web` off, no-project, gitkit-no-repo, no-tool-support model,
  `/pull` preview → `--yes`. Fixed in the audit: `/attach` binary + turn-cap
  refusals point at the model's own read tools / the next turn; `/model <slot>
  <explicit-id>` distinguishes "hidden by `visible_models:`" (supported — this
  is how the m5 capacity model is selected) from "not in this catalog"
  (`/pull`) instead of deferring both to a swap failure a turn later;
  `/backend` health-fail names `/net`, the entry's `api_key_env`, and
  `/planeproxy`; `/tools` states that MCP servers attach at STARTUP ONLY when
  none are attached, since there is no mid-session way to add one. These are
  CHAT-ONLY console strings — benchmark-path error text is untouched.
- `/outage` prints `OUTAGE.md` (the offline emergency card) into the session,
  through the same `luxe.outage.load_card` reader as `luxe outage`. Zero
  network, zero model, zero config: it is the surface that has to work when
  nothing else does. The card's `luxe <sub>` references are asserted against
  the registered CLI commands by a test, so it can't rot.
- `/export`, `/diff`, `/doctor` live in `chat/inspection.py` — logic as testable
  functions, rendering in `commands.py`. All three are READ-ONLY. `/doctor`'s
  RENDERING is `inspection.render_doctor(doc, console, title=…)`, shared with
  `luxe ready` (luxe.sdd) — two renderers would drift, and the point of
  `ready` is that the operator sees the same table outside a session that
  they see in one. `inspection.hostwide_view` restates the session-scoped
  lines (`mode`, `web`, `search index`) for the host-level caller so a
  stand-in session's state is never reported as the user's. `/export`
  renders the PERSISTED transcript (so it works after `/resume` and captures
  interrupted turns) and summarises attachments instead of inlining them;
  default destination is `<session dir>/transcript.md`. `/diff` is git-backed
  and defaults to the files THIS session wrote (ledger `files`), falling back to
  the working tree with the scope named — it diffs against HEAD so staged edits
  show, reports untracked files as new, and survives a repo with no commits.
  `/doctor` returns ordered `Check`s (endpoint → build → key → model → weights
  → manifest → disk → update → index → freshness → tree → mode → TUI); every
  WARN/FAIL carries the fix, an unreachable endpoint is a FAIL not an
  exception, and nothing it calls may raise. The `build` check
  (`luxe.staleproc`, shared with `luxe smoke`) sits directly under the endpoint
  because it EXPLAINS the checks below it: a server left running across a
  `brew upgrade` executes from a deleted Cellar tree, passes health and
  catalog, then fails the next LAZY import with an error naming whatever
  module it reached for — certifi's CA bundle as `[Errno 2]` (2026-08-03), a
  transformers submodule as `ModuleNotFoundError` (2026-08-04, where the
  installed venv demonstrably had it). It is WARN, never FAIL: a stale process
  may still be serving everything asked of it, and the real checks fail on
  their own if it is not. LOCAL endpoints only (a remote host's process table
  is its own doctor's problem) and offline-pure (pgrep/lsof/stat). An
  INCONCLUSIVE verdict — service not running, not brew-installed, lsof mute —
  prints NOTHING rather than a clean bill of health. The `update` check is the ONE
  doctor line allowed to touch the network (a ≤4s `git fetch` on the luxe
  source repo via `buildinfo.fetch_origin`; behind → WARN "`luxe update`");
  offline degrades to a quiet OK "unchecked" — doctor runs during outages by
  design, and session startup stays offline-pure (banner hints use local refs
  only). `search.get_index()` is the read-only accessor it uses — the tool
  path still reads the module global.
- The model roster is CONFIG-FILTERED (2026-07-30): `visible_models:` in
  `configs/chat.yaml` is the only set `/model` offers and slots may resolve to
  (`PipelineConfig.visible`, applied in `SlotManager.available_models`). Server
  order is preserved so `/model <slot> <n>` stays stable; roster ids the server
  doesn't serve are dropped silently. Empty list = show everything (old
  behaviour). Benchmark/maintain never read it.
- Provenance markers are for WIRE CROSSINGS ONLY: ☁ network volume, ⇅ remote
  endpoint. Local weights get NO glyph in `/model` or the status bar — with a
  local-only roster, "if I see it, it is local" (2026-07-30). The legend prints
  only when a marked model is actually listed.
- Tool capability is DETECTED, never assumed (`chat/modelcaps.py`, 2026-07-30).
  A model whose chat template renders no tool calls (gemma-3: roles are
  system/user/assistant, plus an alternation guard) gets the ENTIRE tool surface
  withheld, `session.tools_withheld = True`, and `NO_TOOLS_MODEL_HINT` in the
  frame telling it not to pretend a tool ran. This is not cosmetic: oMLX does not
  reject a request carrying `tools` for such a model, it SILENTLY DROPS them
  (verified live), which would otherwise produce an agent that never calls a tool
  and never says why. Detection reads the local template via oMLX's reported
  `model_path`; a remote endpoint is UNKNOWN and keeps tools ON — a detection gap
  must never disarm the agent.
- `/clear` resets the TURN-DERIVED status too (ctx%, cache, steps, wall, tok/s,
  has_turn) plus pending attachments, via `commands.reset_turn_status`. Window
  size, slot, model, and mode flags survive — those are settings, not history.
  `CommandContext.status` exists for exactly this.
- `/theme preview` renders THIS session's status bar + a tool line + a diff line
  once per palette and restores the active one; `/theme <name>` prints the same
  sample as immediate confirmation. Palette hygiene: `off` states are MUTED (not
  the error role) and the `slot` chip is muted (not blue) — a saturated colour on
  a constant plus red on a safe default was the "red/white/purple/blue" bar.
- Model provenance is always visible (`chat/origin.py`, 2026-07-29): every
  session states at startup whether the chat model's weights sit on local disk
  (`⌂`), on a network volume / cloud-sync tree (`☁`), or behind a remote
  endpoint (`⇅`), the status bar carries that glyph next to the model name
  (warn-coloured when the wire is involved), `/model` labels every listed
  model, and a weight swap names the origin. Resolution is one guarded
  `/v1/models/status` call per endpoint, cached; it happens at startup / on
  `/backend` switch (worker threads) — NEVER during a render, and a failure
  degrades to `unknown` (no glyph), never to a blocked turn.
- The `.sdd` contract scan is cached per repo root for the session
  (`spec_resolver.enable_scan_cache`, enabled in `cli.chat_cmd` only):
  `run_single` rebuilds the contract block every turn and the walk covers the
  whole root — ~19s/turn with `--repo ~`. `finalize_turn` invalidates the entry
  when a turn writes a `.sdd`. The cache is OFF for benchmark/maintain by
  construction: they pin one `--work-dir` across instances with different
  contents, where a path-keyed cache would serve stale contracts.
- Static analyzers (`tools/analysis.py`) resolve via PATH → `python -m <module>`
  → `uvx` (NO auto-install — agent venvs may be frozen) and degrade to a
  SUCCESSFUL machine-readable `{"status":"skipped",…}` when unavailable, so a
  missing linter is never misread as a pass. The `[analyzers]` extra pins the
  toolchain (ruff/mypy/bandit/pip-audit)
- Write tools default OFF (read-only, reusing the `serve`/`make_read_only_role`
  precedent); `/write` toggles; write-mode is shown in the footer every turn
- In read-only mode a lowest-precedence `<session_mode>` block (string =
  `prompts.READ_ONLY_CHAT_HINT`, never inlined here) is injected so the model
  points the user at `/write` instead of claiming luxe can't create/edit files
- `/ctx <tier>` sets a per-session `num_ctx` override (small/medium/large/xlarge);
  it is CHAT-ONLY and always clamped to the role's `num_ctx_max` (config) so a
  request can never exceed what the box/model holds. `num_ctx_max: 0` (default)
  forbids any expansion. Resize is never silent — high context pressure only
  *suggests* the next tier; the user opts in. The benchmark/maintain path never
  reads `num_ctx_max` and is unaffected.
- `/bash` toggles unrestricted shell (default OFF = hardened allowlist). When ON
  AND write mode is on, the turn passes `unrestricted_bash_def()` +
  `make_bash_fn(unrestricted=True)` through `run_single`'s extra-tool seam,
  overriding the allowlisted bash for that run only. Effective only in write mode
  (bash is stripped in read-only). Visible on the slot line every turn. The
  benchmark/maintain path passes no extra tools, so its bash stays allowlisted.
- `luxe chat --dev` starts with write + unrestricted bash ON (sticky equivalent
  of `/write` + `/bash`), so dev work doesn't re-toggle each session
- Write-mode turns swap in `make_prose_aware_write_fns()` (2026-07-30): prose
  files (.txt/.md/…) skip the placeholder honesty guard — saving session
  output / notes containing "# TODO: …" or "# paste your token here" is
  user-dictated content, not a code stub. Code extensions stay guarded; the
  benchmark/maintain path keeps the default guarded fns (tools.sdd).
- `/full` (2026-07-30): re-renders the LAST answer with no display cap. The
  default final render truncates at 50 lines / 4000 chars and `/verbose full`
  only affects FUTURE turns — the truncation hint names `/full` so the hidden
  tail is reachable retroactively (full text always lives in session history;
  no re-run).
- Status-bar ctx% derives from SERVER-reported `last_prompt_tokens /
  effective num_ctx` in BOTH front-ends (repl.py AND tui.py — the tui missed
  the 2026-07-30 fix and read a flat ~7% from the chars/4 estimate, which
  misses tool schemas). The estimate is the fallback only when the server
  reported no usage. The TUI must mirror the corrected value into its live
  `_ctx_pressure` buffer — `_end_busy`'s final `_tick` would otherwise
  overwrite the status with the stale live estimate.
- MCP tools in chat (2026-07-30, cli `--mcp <name>`; repeatable). Servers come
  from the MCP config (`--mcp-config <path>`, default configs/mcp.yaml);
  `streamable_http` transport carries auth via `api_key_env` (credential
  resolved through luxe.secrets: env → ~/.luxe/secrets.env → keychain — the
  VALUE never appears in YAML, same rule as backends). The discovered surface
  is published module-level in `chat/mcptools.py` (like `search.set_index`) and
  injected by `prepare_turn` through the extra-tool seam, so both front-ends
  get it without signature churn. Gating: tools matching the server's
  `gate_tools` fnmatch patterns mutate remote state and follow the /write gate
  exactly like the native mutation surface; `--mcp-read-only` drops them
  entirely. `only_tools` filters discovery. A server that fails to connect is
  reported loudly and skipped — an unreachable relay must not stop a chat
  session from starting (fallback-kit rule), and (2026-07-31) must not stop
  any OTHER server either: connections are ISOLATED PER SERVER, one asyncio
  task and one `AsyncExitStack` each (`mcp/client.py`
  `_async_server_lifetime`). Sharing one stack across servers meant a failing
  transport's anyio `CancelledError` — a BaseException that `except Exception`
  does not catch — escaped and tore down sessions that had already connected:
  `--mcp alpha --mcp kappa` published 0 tools while alpha alone published 20.
  Two invariants follow. (a) The connect boundary catches `BaseException`, not
  `Exception`. (b) A server with no session is DOWN — `start()` sweeps for that
  state and `discover_tools` skips it, so the startup count can never report a
  server "up" that contributed no tools. `down_reason` must name the real cause
  (`_exc_text` flattens anyio's `ExceptionGroup`; a bare cancellation reason is
  provisional and refined by the unwind before `start()` reads it) — "connect
  failed: Cancelled via cancel scope 0x…" tells an operator nothing during an
  outage, which is when this path matters. `/tools` lists the MCP surface
  with its gating. The benchmark/maintain path reads `enabled_for` from
  configs/mcp.yaml as before and never touches `chat/mcptools.py`.
- Web tools (2026-07-31, `/web` + `--web`, default OFF; package `src/luxe/web/`,
  contract `web/web.sdd`). `web_fetch` (bounded HTTP GET → stdlib HTML→markdown
  extraction, `render=true` for headless Chromium) and `web_search` (provider
  key via luxe.secrets; withheld entirely when no key resolves). Injected
  through the extra-tool seam in `prepare_turn` exactly like `net_probe` and the
  MCP surface, so the benchmark/maintain path never sees them — a reproducible
  eval must not be able to reach the live internet. **Gated independently of
  `/write`**: reading a page mutates nothing on disk, and default-OFF is the
  fallback-kit posture (network egress is opted into, not inherited). The
  egress guard (`web/fetch.py`) refuses any host resolving to a non-public
  address on every redirect hop and after JS navigation — `is_private` is NOT
  sufficient, because the tailnet is 100.64.0.0/10 (RFC 6598), which it reports
  as public; `is_global` is the load-bearing predicate. `/tools` lists the web
  surface and what gates it, `/doctor` reports search-key + answers-key +
  render availability without a network call (offline-purity contract
  intact), and the status bar carries a permanent `web on`/`web off` chip
  alongside write/bash (always visible since 2026-08-03 — gate state is
  information, not clutter). `web_answer` (Brave Answers, 2026-08-03) is a
  separate feature from `web_search` on a separate key
  (`BRAVE_ANSWERS_API_KEY`) — see web.sdd.
- `--ctx <tier>` (chat/code startup flag) = `/ctx` before the first turn: sets
  `session.num_ctx_override`, clamped per-turn to the box/model ceiling exactly
  as `/ctx` is. Benchmark/maintain unaffected.
- Defaults stay safe (read-only + allowlisted bash) but the state must be VISIBLE
  and flag-gated failures must be SELF-EXPLAINING: write mode (non-dev) injects
  `restricted_bash_def()` + `make_bash_fn(restricted_hint=True)` so an
  allowlist/chain/substitution rejection front-loads "enable unrestricted dev mode
  with /bash" onto the error (survives the 80-char tool-line truncation) and the
  def tells the model to surface the flag rather than retry. Genuine errors
  (mismatched quotes, timeouts, non-zero exit) are NOT augmented. The startup
  banner names the initial mode + toggles; the status-bar chips show live state.
- The status bar (`chat/status.py`) order (user spec):
  `path · git · ctx · cache · start · last · write · bash · slot · model`.
  `ctx` = `ctx N% <window-size>` (size in the K/M context convention, e.g. 128K;
  the size shows immediately — seeded from the slot's role num_ctx at startup —
  and the % appears once a turn measures usage);
  `cache` = resident prompt size (luxe has no cross-turn prompt cache — each turn
  is a fresh `run_single`, so it's the prompt/KV size processed last turn, NOT an
  Anthropic-style cache); `write`/`bash` show explicit on/off; the luxe mode
  (slot, e.g. `chat`) is its own segment, then the model name pinned last. The
  startup banner is intentionally minimal (session + /help) — repo/slot/model/mode
  are NOT reprinted there since the bar already shows them. `fields()` (→ `list[Segment]`, each
  with a drop-`priority`) is the single source of order/content; `toolbar()`
  (prompt_toolkit bottom bar — its grey/reversed background is overridden to
  `bg:default` so the bar sits on the terminal background), `status_markup()`
  (plain-input fallback), and `to_rich_text()`/`LiveActivity` (in-turn live bar)
  all render from it so they never drift. `fit(segs, width)` is responsive: it
  drops low-value segments (cache=8, start/last=7) highest-first, then
  middle-ellipsises the elastic path; path/git/ctx/model (priority 1–2) protected.
  Git state is TTL-cached to avoid per-keystroke `git` spawns.
- Colours follow the user's ACTIVE Claude statusline theme, resolved LIVE by
  `chat/theme.py`: read the theme name (`CLAUDE_STATUSLINE_THEME` env →
  `~/.claude/statusline-theme` → `claude-dark`), import the user's
  yet-another-statusline `themes` module via the `~/.claude/statusline_command.py`
  symlink, and convert each role's ANSI escape to a (ptk, Rich) pair — ANSI 0-15
  → NAMED colours (track the iTerm2 profile), 16-255 → fixed xterm value,
  rgb/default pass through. The GIT segment is styled by theme ROLE
  (branch/commit/dirty/safe/warn/alert), cached per process; the rest use a
  sparing luxe palette per the user's spec: path BLUE (fixed hex — the terminal's
  ANSI-blue slot renders orange on the user's profile), luxe-mode/slot PURPLE,
  model name YELLOW, state on=GREEN/off=RED, `ctx`/`write`/`bash` LABEL words in
  terminal default fg ("white"/prominent — true white would vanish on the light
  bg), other labels + separators grey, values = default fg. Falls back to a
  built-in llmtop-equivalent ANSI map if the
  repo/symlink/import is absent. NOTE: this reads only the theme-name file and imports the user's pure
  themes module for COLOURS — it is NOT the memory subsystem and injects no
  `~/.claude` content into context (the luxe.sdd/chat.sdd `~/.claude` prohibition
  is scoped to context/memory, not UI theming).
- The interactive front-end is a full-screen **Textual** app (`chat/tui.py`,
  `tui.css`). Layout (top→bottom):

      ┌ RichLog#transcript ─────────── (1fr, scrollable; banner, hint, turns,
      │                                  tool lines, final answers)
      ├ Static#generating ──────────── (transient; spinner · elapsed · tool
      │                                  counts · streamed preview — turn only)
      ├ StatusBar#status ───────────── (status.fields()/fit()/to_rich_text())
      └ Input#prompt ───────────────── (docked at the very bottom)

  The blocking turn runs on a Textual thread worker (`@work(thread=True,
  exclusive=True)` — exclusive + disabling the Input enforces ONE run_single per
  turn). Tool events marshal to the UI thread via `call_from_thread`; tokens
  COALESCE on the worker into a rolling buffer that a UI-thread `set_interval`
  timer (~10 Hz) renders into `#generating` (never per-token `call_from_thread` —
  flood/deadlock). A final frame flushes on completion. The final answer renders
  via `build_final_renderable` into the RichLog. Cancel = key binding (see the
  cancel Must). Front-end selection / fallback:

  | condition                                  | front-end          |
  |--------------------------------------------|--------------------|
  | stdout is a TTY AND `textual` installed    | Textual TUI        |
  | otherwise (CI, pipes, textual-absent)      | line REPL (`repl.py`) |

  `--resume <id>` rides EITHER front-end: the TUI takes a `resume_session_id`
  kwarg and replays the prior transcript into the RichLog on mount
  (`session_store.load_session` + `resume` pairing), seeding the live session's
  turns; `/resume` inside the TUI goes through the same `on_resume` hook (worker
  writes marshal via `call_from_thread`). The textual-absent fallback message
  names the exact fix (`uv sync --extra chat`).

  Paste handling (TUI): stock Textual Input keeps only the FIRST line of a
  paste — silently destructive. `ChatInput` intercepts `Paste` events:
  single-line pastes insert at the cursor as normal; multi-line pastes are
  buffered on the app and shown as a compact `[pasted N lines]` chip in the
  input, expanded back to the full text at submit (chip deleted by the user →
  text appended instead, never dropped). The transcript echoes the chip form;
  the model receives the expanded text. Line endings (2026-07-31): terminals
  emulate KEYSTROKES on paste, so newlines arrive as `\r` — event text is
  normalized (`\r\n`/`\r` → `\n`) BEFORE the multi-line check and the dedup
  compare; detection uses `splitlines()`, never `"\n" in text` (the un-normalized
  check fell through to the stock first-line-only handler: session
  5bb630813c21 pasted a full terminal copy as just its "Last login:" banner).
  Duplicate delivery (2026-07-30): some terminal stacks (tmux/iTerm
  passthrough) deliver ONE clipboard paste as TWO identical `Paste` events;
  an identical paste arriving inside a 1.5s window is dropped (widened from
  0.35s — large pastes parse slower than the old window, which let the
  duplicate through). A deliberate re-paste of the same text lands outside
  the window and inserts normally.

  Input history (TUI, 2026-07-30): up/down cycle previously submitted lines
  readline-style; the in-progress draft is kept and restored when arrowing
  past the newest entry; consecutive duplicates collapse. Lines that
  contained paste chips are NOT recorded (their buffered text is consumed at
  submit — recalling the chip would send the literal chip string).

  Transcript scrollback (TUI, 2026-07-30): the TUI runs on the alternate
  screen, so the TERMINAL/tmux scrollback never sees transcript history —
  in-app keys are the scrollback: PgUp/PgDn (page), shift+↑/↓ (line),
  Home/End (top/bottom), mouse wheel; the RichLog retains 10000 lines. The
  startup banner states this once. The persisted transcript (`/export`)
  remains the full-fidelity record.

  The line REPL keeps its prior behaviour: a `rich.Live` 3-line `LiveActivity`
  (preview · activity · status) when `console.is_terminal`, else `Status` + line
  streaming. Both feed off the chat-only `on_token` seam (`stream=True`); the
  benchmark/maintain path (`on_token=None`, `stream=False`) is untouched.

## Must not
- Modify `agents/loop.py`'s `backend.chat` call site or `run_agent` signature
  (preserves benchmark determinism)
- Introduce any system/task prompt strings here — those stay in
  `agents/prompts.py`. `extra_context` is runtime conversation framing, not a
  registry prompt
- Auto-open PRs from a chat turn (the PR cycle stays in `luxe maintain`/`luxe pr`)
- Let `configs/chat.yaml` change the behavior of `single_64gb.yaml` / maintain

## Owns
- src/luxe/chat/**
